</figure>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related Works:</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work contributes to the growing literature on the computational and learning aspects of Stackelberg games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Korzhyk, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castiglioni et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
In particular,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Letchford et al. (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bacchiocchi et al. (2024)</a></cite> study learning in single-follower
non-Bayesian Stackelberg games. Like our work, they assume that the follower myopically best responds in each round. However, they assume the follower’s utility function to be fixed but unknown, whereas we consider a Bayesian setting in which followers have unknown stochastic types that parameterize a known utility function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Closer to our work,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balcan et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balcan et al. (2025)</a></cite> design online learning algorithms with <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{poly}(K)\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{poly}(K)\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math> regrets for Bayesian Stackelberg games with a single follower with unknown type distribution, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernasconi et al. (2023)</a></cite> obtain <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{3n/2}\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{3n/2}\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret for multi-receiver Bayesian persuasion problem (which is similar to multi-follower Bayesian Stackelberg game) by a reduction to adversarial linear bandit problem. Adopting previous techniques would lead to a <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{poly}(K^{n})\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{poly}(K^{n})\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math> regret bound in our multi-follower setting, which is exponential in the number of followers <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (see details in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and undesirable when followers are many.
Using a different approach, we design an algorithm with <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT})</annotation></semantics></math> regret, a better result when the number of leader’s actions <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is small compared to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
The exponential dependency on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is unavoidable from a computational perspective, as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm (2006)</a></cite> show that BSGs are NP-Hard to solve with respect to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our algorithm combines the Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) principle and a partition of the leader’s strategy space into best-response regions, which is a novel approach to our knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Online Bayesian Stackelberg game can be seen as a piecewise linear stochastic bandit problem. While linear stochastic bandit problems have been well studied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Auer et al., 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dani et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbasi-yadkori et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, piecewise linearity brings additional challenges. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bacchiocchi et al. (2025)</a></cite> study a single-dimensional piecewise linear stochastic bandit problem with unknown pieces; in contrast, we have known pieces but a multi-dimensional space, so the techniques and results of that work and ours are not directly comparable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Model</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game:</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the interactions between a single <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">leader</span> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">followers</span>. The leader has <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> actions, denoted by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=[L]=\{1,\ldots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=[L]=\{1,\ldots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and chooses a mixed strategy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> over them, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of probability distributions over the action set. We use <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the probability of the leader playing action <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each follower has a finite action set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=[A]</annotation></semantics></math>. We represent the joint action of the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> followers as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}=(a_{1},...,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}=(a_{1},...,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each follower <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> also has a private type <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}\in\Theta=[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}\in\Theta=[K]</annotation></semantics></math>, with the vector of all follower types denoted by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}=(\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n})\in\Theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}=(\theta_{1},...,\theta_{n})\in\Theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider a Bayesian setting where this type vector is drawn from a distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>), with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the marginal distribution of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The properties of this joint distribution play a key role in our results. We will consider two scenarios:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independent type distributions: The followers’ types are independent: <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}=\mathcal{D}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{D}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}=\mathcal{D}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathcal{D}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">General type distributions: The followers’ types can be arbitrarily correlated.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If the leader selects action <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and the followers select joint action <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, the leader receives utility <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\ell,\bm{a})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\ell,\bm{a})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and each follower <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> receives utility <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(\ell,a_{i},\theta_{i})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(\ell,a_{i},\theta_{i})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that each follower’s utility depends only on their own action and type, alongside the leader’s action; it does not depend on the actions of other followers.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            This no externality assumption is common in modeling a large population of agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dughmi and Xu, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castiglioni et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</span></span></span>
For a mixed strategy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> and followers’ actions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, the leader’s expected utility is given by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,\bm{a})=\mathbb{E}_{\ell\sim x}[u(\ell,\bm{a})]=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)u(\ell,\bm{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,\bm{a})=\mathbb{E}_{\ell\sim x}[u(\ell,\bm{a})]=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)u(\ell,\bm{a})</annotation></semantics></math>. Likewise, the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th follower’s expected utility under <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}(x,a_{i},\theta_{i})=\mathbb{E}_{\ell\sim x}[v_{i}(\ell,a_{i},\theta)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}(x,a_{i},\theta_{i})=\mathbb{E}_{\ell\sim x}[v_{i}(\ell,a_{i},\theta)]</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the leader knows each follower’s utility function but not their private types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An instance of a multi-follower Bayesian Stackelberg game is defined by the tuple <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=(n,L,A,K,u,v,\bm{\mathcal{D}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=(n,L,A,K,u,v,\bm{\mathcal{D}})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this game, the leader first commits to a mixed strategy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> without knowledge of the followers’ types. The follower types are then jointly realized from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each follower selects a <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">best-responding</em> action based on the leader’s strategy. It is without loss of generality to consider followers choosing pure action since follower utilities are independent of one another.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Followers’ Best Response)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a leader’s mixed strategy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the best response of a follower <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with realized type <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{br}_{i}(\theta_{i},x)\in\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}v_{i}(x,a,\theta_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>br</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{br}_{i}(\theta_{i},x)\in\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}v_{i}(x,a,\theta_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">In case of ties, we assume that followers break ties in favor of the leader.</span></span></span></span>
The vector of best responses is denoted by <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=(\mathrm{br}_{1}(\theta_{1},x),...,\mathrm{br}_{n}(\theta_{n},x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>br</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>br</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=(\mathrm{br}_{1}(\theta_{1},x),...,\mathrm{br}_{n}(\theta_{n},x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the leader’s expected utility when the leader commits to mixed strategy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the followers have their types drawn from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> and best respond.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Leader’s Optimal Strategy)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a joint follower type distribution <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the leader’s optimal strategy, also known as the Stackelberg Equilibrium, is given as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{*}~\in~\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)~=~\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}{\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.608em">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{*}~\in~\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)~=~\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}{\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Online Learning Model:</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the leader knows the distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, they can compute the optimal strategy by solving the optimization problem specified in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Leader’s Optimal Strategy). ‣ Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game: ‣ 2 Model ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Indeed, this is the premise of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm (2006)</a></cite>. Our work examines an online learning model where the leader does not know the type distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> a priori; instead, the leader must learn the optimal strategy through feedback from repeated interactions with followers over <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We examine two feedback models. In the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">type feedback</span> setting, the leader observes the types <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the followers after each round <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas in the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">action feedback</span> setting, the leader only observes the actions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the followers. Note that type feedback is strictly more informative than action feedback since the follower’s actions can be inferred from their types by computing their best response (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 2.1 (Followers’ Best Response). ‣ Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game: ‣ 2 Model ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We summarize the interactions at a given round <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The leader chooses a strategy <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Follower types for this round are realized: <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Followers take their best-responding actions <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{t}={\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{t}={\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and the leader gets utility <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x^{t},\bm{a}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x^{t},\bm{a}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under type feedback, the leader observes the type profile <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under action feedback, the leader observes only the followers’ actions <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The leader deploys a learning algorithm (based on past feedback) to select strategy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for every round <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We study learning algorithms that minimize the cumulative <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regret</em> with respect to the optimal equilibrium strategy (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Leader’s Optimal Strategy). ‣ Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game: ‣ 2 Model ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). Formally defined below, minimizing this objective requires a careful balance between exploring the strategy space while not taking too many sub-optimal strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">regret</em> of a learning algorithm that selects strategy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}(T)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}{\Big[u(x^{*},{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{t},x^{*}))-u(x^{t},{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{t},x^{t}))\Big]}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Big(U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x^{*})-U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x^{t})\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Reg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}(T)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}{\Big[u(x^{*},{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{t},x^{*}))-u(x^{t},{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{t},x^{t}))\Big]}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Big(U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x^{*})-U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x^{t})\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Reg}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Reg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Reg}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable, because the selection of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the past type realizations <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We aim to minimize the expected regret <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Reg}(T)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Reg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Reg}(T)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, our model assumes followers behave <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">myopically</span>, selecting their best actions based only on the leader’s current strategy, without considering future rounds. This is consistent with the related literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bacchiocchi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Letchford et al., 2009</a>)</cite> and well-motivated in settings like online platforms or security games where followers maximize their immediate utility.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the followers’ best-responding actions are sensitive to the leader’s strategy <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,
the leader’s expected utility function <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is discontinuous in <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. This presents a key challenge to both learning and optimizing over the leader’s strategy space. To overcome this challenge, we first show that the leader’s strategy space <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into a polynomial number of non-empty <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">best-response regions</em> (followers have the same best-response actions within each region).
While the notion of best-response regions has been proposed by prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balcan et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bacchiocchi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Zhang, 2024</a>)</cite>, those works consider single-follower cases.
With multiple followers, we will argue that the number of such regions does not increase exponentially in the number of followers <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) – a key property to be used in later sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>A Single Follower</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To build intuition, we first consider the leader playing against a single follower <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n=1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The follower has a utility function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\ell,a,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\ell,a,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and a type <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta=[K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta=[K]</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:\Theta\to\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:\Theta\to\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a mapping from follower type to action – i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> specifies an action for type <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. For such a mapping <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(w)\subseteq\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(w)\subseteq\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of leader strategies under which the follower’s best response action <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{br}(\theta,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{br}(\theta,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> for every type <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~\mathrm{br}(\theta,x)=w(\theta),\,\,\forall\theta\in\Theta\Big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.497em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~\mathrm{br}(\theta,x)=w(\theta),\,\,\forall\theta\in\Theta\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~v(x,w(\theta),\theta)\geq v(x,a^{\prime},\theta),\,\,\forall\theta\in\Theta,\forall a^{\prime}\in\mathcal{A}\Big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.497em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~v(x,w(\theta),\theta)\geq v(x,a^{\prime},\theta),\,\,\forall\theta\in\Theta,\forall a^{\prime}\in\mathcal{A}\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where we recall that for any action <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x,a,\theta)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}{x(\ell)v(\ell,a,\theta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x,a,\theta)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}{x(\ell)v(\ell,a,\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(w)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">best-response region</em> for mapping <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. This region can also equivalently be defined as the intersection of several halfspaces (see Figure <a href="#A1.F1.fig1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Appendix A Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for a visual). In particular, let</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}}=\big[v(1,a,\theta)-v(1,a^{\prime},\theta)\,\,,\,\,\dots\,\,,\,\,v(L,a,\theta)-v(L,a^{\prime},\theta)\big]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}}=\big[v(1,a,\theta)-v(1,a^{\prime},\theta)\,\,,\,\,\dots\,\,,\,\,v(L,a,\theta)-v(L,a^{\prime},\theta)\big]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">denote the “advantage” of follower type <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> taking action <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> at each of the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> possible leader actions. Then the halfspace <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}})=\big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})\,|\,\langle x,d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}}\rangle\geq 0\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}})=\big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})\,|\,\langle x,d_{\theta,a,a^{\prime}}\rangle\geq 0\big\}</annotation></semantics></math> contains all the leader strategies under which the follower with type <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> prefers action <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the best-response region is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(w)~=~\bigcap_{\theta\in\Theta,a\in\mathcal{A}}H(d_{\theta,w(\theta),a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.441em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(w)~=~\bigcap_{\theta\in\Theta,a\in\mathcal{A}}H(d_{\theta,w(\theta),a})</annotation></semantics></math>, the intersection of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Theta|\cdot|\mathcal{A}|=KA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Theta|\cdot|\mathcal{A}|=KA</annotation></semantics></math> halfspaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Multiple Followers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generalize the intuitions from the single-follower case to the multi-follower case. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> mappings, where each <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}:\Theta\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}:\Theta\to A</annotation></semantics></math> is the best-response mapping for follower <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Alternatively, one can think of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> as a matrix, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=[w_{ik}]\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=[w_{ik}]\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each entry <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> records the best-response action for follower <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> if he has type <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}=k</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a joint type <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}=(\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}=(\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of all followers, we use <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(\bm{\theta})=(w_{1}(\theta_{1}),\ldots,w_{n}(\theta_{n}))\in\mathcal{A}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\bm{\theta})=(w_{1}(\theta_{1}),\ldots,w_{n}(\theta_{n}))\in\mathcal{A}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the joint action of all followers under this type profile. We generalize the notion of best-response region <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(w)</annotation></semantics></math> from the singe-follower case to the multi-follower case:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Best-Response Region)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a matrix <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">best-response region for <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math></em> is the set of leader strategies under which the followers’ best responses are given by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta}),\quad\forall\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}\Big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.497em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~=~\Big\{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})~\big|~{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta}),\quad\forall\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As in the single-follower case, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as the intersection of multiple halfspaces: <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)=\bigcap_{i\in[n],\theta_{i}\in\Theta,a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}}H(d_{\theta_{i},w_{i}(\theta_{i}),a_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)=\bigcap_{i\in[n],\theta_{i}\in\Theta,a_{i}\in\mathcal{A}}H(d_{\theta_{i},w_{i}(\theta_{i}),a_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We make an important observation: the leader’s expected utility function <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is linear in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> within each non-empty best-response region. By definition, for all <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. So,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,\bm{\mathrm{br}}(\bm{\theta},x))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)u(\ell,W(\bm{\theta}))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})\langle x,z_{W,\bm{\theta}}\rangle." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,\bm{\mathrm{br}}(\bm{\theta},x))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)u(\ell,W(\bm{\theta}))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})\langle x,z_{W,\bm{\theta}}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{W,\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{W,\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{W,\bm{\theta}}=(u(1,W(\bm{\theta})),...,u(L,W(\bm{\theta})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{W,\bm{\theta}}=(u(1,W(\bm{\theta})),...,u(L,W(\bm{\theta})))</annotation></semantics></math>. So, we conclude that the leader’s expected utility is linear within each region <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, the leader’s expected utility function <math id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is linear in <math id="S3.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is linear <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within</em> each best-response region, it could be non-linear and even discontinuous <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across</em> different best-response regions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\{W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}~|~R(W)\neq\emptyset\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\{W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}~|~R(W)\neq\emptyset\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of mappings <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> for which the corresponding best-response region <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty. Although the total number of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{A}^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{n\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{n\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-empty</em> best-response regions is significantly smaller, especially when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (number of actions of the leader) is treated as a constant. The exact characterization is given below.
The proof (in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) uses a result in computational geometry regarding the number of
nonempty regions obtained by dividing <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nKA^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nKA^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> hyperplanes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The number of non-empty best-response regions, <math id="S3.Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{W}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{W}|</annotation></semantics></math>, is <math id="S3.Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any algorithm to leverage these best response regions, it is imperative that these regions can be enumerated efficiently. The following lemma shows this is always possible. Intuitively, we construct a graph where the nodes represent non-empty best-response regions and an edge exists between <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,W^{\prime}\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,W^{\prime}\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> differ in exactly one entry. Traversing an edge, therefore, corresponds to moving to an adjacent best-response region by crossing a single hyperplane boundary. We show that this graph is always connected and can thus be efficiently traversed using breadth-first search. The exact algorithm and proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> are in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of non-empty best-response regions <math id="S3.Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{R(W):W\in\mathcal{W}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R(W):W\in\mathcal{W}\}</annotation></semantics></math> can be enumerated in <math id="S3.Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}(n^{L},K^{L},A^{L},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}(n^{L},K^{L},A^{L},L)</annotation></semantics></math> time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the optimal strategy within each region can be efficiently computed. Recall from Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Leader’s Optimal Strategy). ‣ Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game: ‣ 2 Model ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> that, when given the followers’ type distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the leader’s optimal strategy requires solving <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}{\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}{\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the leader’s utility is linear within a region <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal solution within <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by the following linear program:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{x\in R(W)}\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}{\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}))}=\max_{x\in R(W)}\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}{\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,W(\bm{\theta}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{x\in R(W)}\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}{\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}))}=\max_{x\in R(W)}\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}{\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,W(\bm{\theta}))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the following set of linear constraints:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)\big[v_{i}(\ell,w_{i}(\theta_{i}),\theta_{i})-v_{i}(\ell,a^{\prime}_{i},\theta_{i})\big]\geq 0,~~\forall i\in[n],~\forall\theta_{i}\in\Theta,~\forall a^{\prime}_{i}\in\mathcal{A},\\
x(\ell)\geq 0,~\forall\ell\in\mathcal{L},~\text{ and }~\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)=1.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.497em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)\big[v_{i}(\ell,w_{i}(\theta_{i}),\theta_{i})-v_{i}(\ell,a^{\prime}_{i},\theta_{i})\big]\geq 0,~~\forall i\in[n],~\forall\theta_{i}\in\Theta,~\forall a^{\prime}_{i}\in\mathcal{A},\\
x(\ell)\geq 0,~\forall\ell\in\mathcal{L},~\text{ and }~\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}}x(\ell)=1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">While there are <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nKA)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nKA)</annotation></semantics></math> constraints, each involving a sum over <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> elements, the objective involves summing over <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> possible type profiles. While this is exponential in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, any input to the complete information instance must provide the joint type distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}\in[0,1]^{K^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}\in[0,1]^{K^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> as input. Thus, the time to compute the optimal solution within each region is polynomial in the input size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above results imply that, given distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal leader strategy in BSGs can be computed efficiently when the number <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of leader’s actions is small.
This is because the optimal strategy within each best-response region <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed efficiently by the linear program equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the overall optimal strategy is the maximum over all non-empty best-response regions, and there are at most <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})</annotation></semantics></math> such regions by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
In comparison, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm (2006)</a></cite> prove that the optimal strategy is NP-hard to compute in BSGs with an asymptotically increasing <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
We showed above that BSGs are polynomial-time solvable for a constant <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Type Feedback</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General Type Distributions:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now address the core problem of learning the optimal leader strategy from online feedback. This section considers the type-feedback setting, where the leader observes each follower’s realized type <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t}=(\theta^{t}_{1},\dots,\theta^{t}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t}=(\theta^{t}_{1},\dots,\theta^{t}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> at the end of round <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We start with general distributions – that is, the followers’ types can be arbitrarily correlated.
Observing types after each round allows us to directly estimate the unknown distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute an optimal strategy accordingly. This is formalized in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALGORITHM 1</span> </span>Type-Feedback Algorithm – General Type Distributions</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
At round <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, pick an arbitrary strategy <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{1}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{1}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">round <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Choose <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))</annotation></semantics></math> -- the empirically optimal strategy. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Observe the followers’ types <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{t}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At first glance, one might think that this algorithm might suffer a large regret because the distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has support size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Theta^{n}|=K^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Theta^{n}|=K^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is difficult to estimate. Indeed, the estimation error for such a distribution using <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> samples is at least <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\big(\sqrt{\tfrac{K^{n}}{t}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\big(\sqrt{\tfrac{K^{n}}{t}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> even if <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a product distribution (namely, the types are independent) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, 2022</a>)</cite>. This suggests that the empirically optimal strategy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> might be worse than the true optimal strategy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> by at least <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\big(\sqrt{\tfrac{K^{n}}{t}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\big(\sqrt{\tfrac{K^{n}}{t}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, which would cause an <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{K^{n}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{K^{n}T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds in total. As we will show in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, one analysis of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> achieves exactly this as a regret upper bound. The proof (in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>) upper bounds the single-round regret by the total variation (TV) distance between the empirical distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the true distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While this suggests that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret might be tight, this is interestingly not true when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large! That is, the intuitive lower bound that arises from the estimation error for distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not correct. Although the empirical type distribution can differ significantly from the true type distribution, the empirical <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utility</em> of any strategy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is actually concentrated around the true expected utility of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability. We formalize this below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{1},\ldots,\bm{\theta}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝛉</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{1},\ldots,\bm{\theta}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from distribution <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}^{t}(x)=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{s=1}^{t}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝛉</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}^{t}(x)=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{s=1}^{t}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))</annotation></semantics></math> be the empirical expected utility of a strategy <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> computed on the <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Recall that <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\bm{\mathcal{D}}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the true expected utility of <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. With probability at least <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we have: for all <math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.Thmlemma1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)-\hat{U}^{t}(x)\big|\leq\mathcal{O}\Big(\sqrt{\tfrac{L\log t}{t}}+\sqrt{\tfrac{L\log(nKA)+\log(1/\delta)}{t}}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|U_{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}(x)-\hat{U}^{t}(x)\big|\leq\mathcal{O}\Big(\sqrt{\tfrac{L\log t}{t}}+\sqrt{\tfrac{L\log(nKA)+\log(1/\delta)}{t}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the above concentration result holds for all strategies <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously, instead of for a single fixed strategy (which easily follows from Hoeffding’s inequality).
The proof of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> (in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) relies on an analysis of the pseudo-dimension of the leader’s utility functions and leverages insights about the best-response regions (discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
This result means that the simple Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can achieve a regret that is of the order <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, logarithmic in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, with an additional <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math> factor.
This is better for large <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
This new regret bound, along with the earlier one <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})</annotation></semantics></math>, is formalized in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> below, with the proof given in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The type-feedback Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for general type distributions achieves expected regret <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\big(\min\big\{\sqrt{LT\cdot\log(nKAT)},\sqrt{K^{n}T}\big\}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\big(\min\big\{\sqrt{LT\cdot\log(nKAT)},\sqrt{K^{n}T}\big\}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> and can be implemented in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LT)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LT)</annotation></semantics></math> time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> also comments on the runtime of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which hinges on the computability of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta}^{s},x))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the techniques developed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, this maximization can be solved by taking the maximum over the optimal strategies from each non-empty best-response region <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, computed using the empirical type distribution.
Using Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and the fact that the optimal strategy within a non-empty <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> can be solved by the following linear program, we obtain a runtime that is polynomial when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is constant:<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              Also note that Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> does not need as input the entire utility function of the leader <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, which has an exponential size <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\cdot A^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\cdot A^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The algorithm only needs the utility function for the sampled types.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{W\in\mathcal{W}}\left\{\max_{x\in R(W)}{\sum_{s=1}^{t}{u(x,W(\bm{\theta}^{s}))}}~~\text{subject to the constraints in (\ref{eq:opt_strat_cons})}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>subject to the constraints in (</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{W\in\mathcal{W}}\left\{\max_{x\in R(W)}{\sum_{s=1}^{t}{u(x,W(\bm{\theta}^{s}))}}~~\text{subject to the constraints in (\ref{eq:opt_strat_cons})}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Independent Type Distributions:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the corresponding regret bound in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> hold without any assumptions on the joint type distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In many settings, however, the followers’ types may be independent of one another. Intuitively, one expects learning to be easier in such settings since it suffices to learn the marginals as opposed to the richer joint distribution. This is indeed correct: in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In Independent Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we build the empirical distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for each marginal from samples <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}^{1},\ldots,\theta_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}^{1},\ldots,\theta_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for follower <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and then take the product <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALGORITHM 2</span> </span>Type-Feedback Algorithm - Independent Type Distributions </figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">At <math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, pick an arbitrary strategy <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{1}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{1}\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">round <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Choose <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t-1}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}\in\argmax_{x\in\Delta(\mathcal{L})}\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\theta}\sim\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t-1}}[u(x,{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x))]</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Observe realized follower type <math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\theta^{t}_{1},\dots,\theta^{t}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\theta^{t}_{1},\dots,\theta^{t}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}(k)=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{s=1}^{t}\mathds{1}\left[\theta^{s}_{i}=k\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}(k)=\frac{1}{t}\sum_{s=1}^{t}\mathds{1}\left[\theta^{s}_{i}=k\right]</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="algorithm2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}(\bm{\theta})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}(\theta_{i}),~\forall\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}(\bm{\theta})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}(\theta_{i}),~\forall\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This algorithm achieves a much improved regret, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{nKT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{nKT})</annotation></semantics></math>, formalized in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ Independent Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and empirically verified in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Simulations ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. The proof (in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>) is similar to the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K^{n}T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret analysis of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which upper bounds the single-round regret by the TV distance between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
But for independent distributions, we can relate the TV distance with the sum of Hellinger distances between the marginals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{D}}^{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{D}}^{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is bounded by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{nK}{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{nK}{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{K^{n}}{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>t</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{K^{n}}{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the total regret is bounded by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{nKT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{nKT})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The computational complexity, though, increases to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LTK^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LTK^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as the empirical product distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}=\prod_{i}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\mathcal{D}}}^{t}=\prod_{i}^{n}\hat{\mathcal{D}}_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has support size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The type-feedback Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In Independent Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for independent type distributions achieves expected regret <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\big(\sqrt{nKT}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\big(\sqrt{nKT}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> and can be implemented in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LTK^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}((nKA)^{L}LTK^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Taking the minimum of Theorems <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ Independent Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we obtain a type-feedback algorithm with expected regret <math id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\big(\min\big\{\sqrt{LT\cdot\log(nKAT)},\sqrt{nKT}\big\}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\big(\min\big\{\sqrt{LT\cdot\log(nKAT)},\sqrt{nKT}\big\}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> for independent type distributions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Lower Bound</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then provide a lower bound result: no algorithm for online Bayesian Stackelberg game has a better regret than <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,nK\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,nK\}T})</annotation></semantics></math>. When the number of followers <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large, this lower bound matches the previous upper bounds <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{LT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{LT})</annotation></semantics></math>. To our knowledge, this work is the first to provide a lower bound for the multi-follower problem and give an almost tight characterization of the factor before the classical <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math> term. Interestingly, this <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{L})</annotation></semantics></math> factor does not grow with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> up to log factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The expected regret of any type-feedback algorithm is at least <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,nK\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,nK\}T})</annotation></semantics></math>. This holds even if the followers’ types are independent and the leader’s utility does not depend <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof (given in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Ω(√min{{𝐿,𝑛⁢𝐾}⁢𝑇}) Lower Bound for the Multi-Follower Case: Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>) involves two non-trivial reductions. First, we reduce the <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution learning</em> problem to a <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-follower</em> Bayesian Stackelberg game, obtaining an <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,K\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,K\}T})</annotation></semantics></math> lower bound. Then, we reduce the single-follower game with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nK</annotation></semantics></math> types to a game with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> followers each with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> types. One might wish to reduce a single-follower game with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> types to an <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-follower game to prove a lower bound of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,K^{n}\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(\sqrt{\min\{L,K^{n}\}T})</annotation></semantics></math> for general type distributions, but that cannot be done easily due to no externality between the followers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Action Feedback</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now discuss the setting where the leader observes the followers’ actions after each round.
This setting is more practical yet challenging than the type-feedback setting.
We present two learning algorithms.
The first algorithm achieves <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> regret, using a previous technique from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernasconi et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
The second algorithm involves a novel combination of the Upper Confidence Bound principle and the concentration analysis of best-response regions from Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, achieving <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT\log T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret.
The latter is better when the number of followers <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large and the number of leader actions <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is small. We empirically simulate both approaches in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Simulations ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear-bandit based approach with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> regret:</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernasconi et al. (2023)</a></cite> developed a technique to reduce the online learning problem for a linear program with unknown objective parameter to a linear bandit problem.
Although the optimization problem for Bayesian Stackelberg game (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Leader’s Optimal Strategy). ‣ Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Game: ‣ 2 Model ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) is not a linear program, we can still apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernasconi et al. (2023)</a></cite>’s technique.
We first show that, under a different formulation, Bayesian Stackelberg game can actually be solved by a single linear program.
Then, we reduce the linear program formulation of online Bayesian Stackelberg game to a linear bandit problem. A difference between our work and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernasconi et al. (2023)</a></cite> is that, while they consider an adversarial online learning environment, we have a stochastic environment. Directly applying their technique will lead to a sub-optimal <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{\frac{3n}{2}}\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{\frac{3n}{2}}\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret bound. Instead, we apply the OFUL algorithm for stochastic linear bandit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbasi-yadkori et al., 2011</a>)</cite> to obtain a better regret bound of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{n}\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(K^{n}\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. See details in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 𝒪(𝐾^𝑛⁢√𝑇⁢log𝑇)-Regret Algorithm and the Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an action-feedback algorithm for online Bayesian Stackelberg game with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> regret.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm3" title="In Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}LT\log T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a better algorithm for large <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and small <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall from Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that the leader’s strategy space can be partitioned into best-response regions: <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{L})=\bigcup_{W\in\mathcal{W}}R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{L})=\bigcup_{W\in\mathcal{W}}R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. When the leader plays strategy <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in a region <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, the followers’ best-response function satisfies <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mathrm{br}}}(\bm{\theta},x)=W(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the leader’s expected utility is</p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U(x,R(W))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,W(\bm{\theta}))=\sum_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}}u(x,\bm{a})\sum_{\bm{\theta}|W(\bm{\theta})=\bm{a}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})=\sum_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}}u(x,\bm{a})\mathcal{P}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U(x,R(W))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})u(x,W(\bm{\theta}))=\sum_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}}u(x,\bm{a})\sum_{\bm{\theta}|W(\bm{\theta})=\bm{a}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})=\sum_{\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}}u(x,\bm{a})\mathcal{P}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}:W(\bm{\theta})=\bm{a}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))=\sum_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta^{n}:W(\bm{\theta})=\bm{a}}\bm{\mathcal{D}}(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability that the followers jointly take action <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}\in\mathcal{A}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> when the leader plays <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the distribution <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))\in\Delta(\mathcal{A}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as long as <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, playing <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> strategies <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{1},...,x^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{1},...,x^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> observations <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{a}^{1},...,\bm{a}^{N}\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{1},...,\bm{a}^{N}\sim\mathcal{P}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using these samples, we can estimate the utility of any other strategy <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> within the same region. We define the empirical utility estimate on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples of joint actions as
<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}_{N}(x,R(W))~=~\frac{1}{N}\sum_{s=1}^{N}u(x,\bm{a}^{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}_{N}(x,R(W))~=~\frac{1}{N}\sum_{s=1}^{N}u(x,\bm{a}^{s})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq|\mathcal{W}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq|\mathcal{W}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
With probability at least <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{1}{T^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{1}{T^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have: <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall N\in\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall N\in\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|U(x,R(W))-\hat{U}_{N}(x,R(W))|\leq\sqrt{\tfrac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|U(x,R(W))-\hat{U}_{N}(x,R(W))|\leq\sqrt{\tfrac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this lemma is similar to the proof of Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ General Type Distributions: ‣ 4.1 Learning Algorithms and Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix C Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each region <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N^{t}(W)=\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[x^{s}\in R(W)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{t}(W)=\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[x^{s}\in R(W)\right]</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of times when strategies in region <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> were played in the first <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math> rounds.
Given the result in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, we define an Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) on the expected utility of the optimal strategy in region <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)~=~\max_{x\in R(W)}\Big\{\hat{U}_{N^{t}(W)}(x,R(W))\Big\}~+~\sqrt{\tfrac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N^{t}(W)}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>UCB</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.552em">+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)~=~\max_{x\in R(W)}\Big\{\hat{U}_{N^{t}(W)}(x,R(W))\Big\}~+~\sqrt{\tfrac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N^{t}(W)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We design the following algorithm: at each round <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, select the region <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> with the highest <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>UCB</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, play the empirically optimal strategy in that region, and increment <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{t}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{t}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> by 1. Full description of the algorithm is given in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm3" title="In Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm3" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALGORITHM 3</span> </span>Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) for Best-Response Regions</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm3.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
Let <math id="algorithm3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\{W\mid R(W)\neq\emptyset\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>W</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\{W\mid R(W)\neq\emptyset\}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm3.4.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm3.4.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Choose any strategy <math id="algorithm3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> and observe a joint action. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm3.4.4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">round <math id="algorithm3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;|\mathcal{W}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;|\mathcal{W}|</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm3.4.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm3.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm3.4.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">each <math id="algorithm3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm3.4.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Let <math id="algorithm3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N^{t}(W)=\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[W^{s}=W\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{t}(W)=\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[W^{s}=W\right]</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of times region <math id="algorithm3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen.
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Let <math id="algorithm3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\,|\,R(W))</annotation></semantics></math> be the empirical distribution of joint actions in the rounds where region <math id="algorithm3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen: <math id="algorithm3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))=\frac{1}{N^{t}(W)}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[W^{s}=W\right]\cdot\mathds{1}\left[\bm{a}^{s}=\bm{a}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\bm{a}\,|\,R(W))=\frac{1}{N^{t}(W)}\sum_{s=1}^{t-1}\mathds{1}\left[W^{s}=W\right]\cdot\mathds{1}\left[\bm{a}^{s}=\bm{a}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Compute the empirically optimal strategy in region <math id="algorithm3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(W)</annotation></semantics></math>:

<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hskip-40.00006pt\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W)}~=~\argmax_{x\in R(W)}\,\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\mid R(W))}\big[u(x,\bm{a})\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.608em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hskip-40.00006pt\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W)}~=~\argmax_{x\in R(W)}\,\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\mid R(W))}\big[u(x,\bm{a})\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>which has empirical utility <math id="algorithm3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{u}^{*}_{R(W)}=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\mid R(W))}[u(\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W)},\bm{a})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}^{*}_{R(W)}=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim\hat{\mathcal{P}}^{t}(\cdot\mid R(W))}[u(\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W)},\bm{a})]</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Let <math id="algorithm3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)=\hat{u}^{*}_{R(W)}+\sqrt{\frac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N^{t}(W)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>UCB</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)=\hat{u}^{*}_{R(W)}+\sqrt{\frac{4(L+1)\log(3T)}{N^{t}(W)}}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Let <math id="algorithm3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}\in\arg\max_{W\in\mathcal{W}}{\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>UCB</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}\in\arg\max_{W\in\mathcal{W}}{\mathrm{UCB}^{t}(W)}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Play strategy <math id="algorithm3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}=\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W^{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}=\hat{x}^{*}_{R(W^{t})}</annotation></semantics></math> and observe joint action <math id="algorithm3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}^{t}=(a^{t}_{1},\dots,a^{t}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}^{t}=(a^{t}_{1},\dots,a^{t}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="algorithm3.4.12" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm3" title="In Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> has expected regret <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\big(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L\cdot T\log T}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\big(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L\cdot T\log T}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While the full proof is in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Appendix for Section ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>, we sketch the intuition. In the classical multi-armed bandit problem, the UCB algorithm has expected regret <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{mT\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{mT\log T})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of arms. In our setting, each best-response region corresponds to an arm, and the confidence bound for each region is <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{L\log T}{N^{t}(W)}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{\frac{L\log T}{N^{t}(W)}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of arms/regions is <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|\mathcal{W}|=\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|\mathcal{W}|=\mathcal{O}(n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L})</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.3 Enumerating Best-Response Regions and Computing the Offline Optimal ‣ 3 Best Response Regions: A Geometric Perspective ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. So, the regret of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm3" title="In Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is at most <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{|\mathcal{W}|\cdot L\cdot T\log T})=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}\cdot L\cdot T\log T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{|\mathcal{W}|\cdot L\cdot T\log T})=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}\cdot L\cdot T\log T})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By taking the better algorithm in Theorems <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ Linear-bandit based approach with 𝒪(𝐾^𝑛⁢√𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret: ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ Algorithm with 𝑂(√𝑛^𝐿⁢𝐾^𝐿⁢𝐴^{2⁢𝐿}⁢𝐿⁢𝑇⁢log𝑇) regret. ‣ 5 Action Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we obtain an action-feedback algorithm with <math id="S5.Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\min\big\{K^{n},\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L}\big\}\sqrt{T}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\min\big\{K^{n},\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L}\big\}\sqrt{T}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> regret.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dependencies on various parameters:</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since action-feedback is more limited than type-feedback, the lower bound in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.2 Lower Bound ‣ 4 Type Feedback ‣ Learning to Play Multi-Follower Bayesian Stackelberg Games" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> immediately carries over and shows that the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> regret bounds here are tight in <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. There are several subtleties in achieving tighter bounds on the remaining parameters. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm (2006)</a></cite> show that, even with known distributions, BSG are NP-Hard to solve with respect to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>; so an exponential computational dependence on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is unavoidable, even if the regret could be made independent of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in our <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(K^{n}\sqrt{T}\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> result.
Whether an online learning algorithm with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{poly}(n,K,L)\sqrt{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{poly}(n,K,L)\sqrt{T}</annotation></semantics></math> regret exists is an open question, but such an algorithm will suffer an exponential runtime in <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> unless P = NP.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work designed online learning algorithms for Bayesian Stackelberg games with multiple followers with unknown type distributions. Although the joint type space of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> followers has an exponentially large size <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we achieved significantly smaller regrets: <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\min\{nK,L\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\min\{nK,L\}T})</annotation></semantics></math> when the followers’ types are independent and observable, <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\min\{K^{n},L\}T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{\min\{K^{n},L\}T})</annotation></semantics></math> when followers’ types are correlated and observable, and <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\min\{K^{n},\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L}\}\sqrt{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\min\{K^{n},\sqrt{n^{L}K^{L}A^{2L}L}\}\sqrt{T})</annotation></semantics></math> when only the followers’ actions are observed.
These results exploit various geometric properties of the leader’s strategy space.
The type-feedback bounds are tight in all parameters and the action-feedback bounds are tight in <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
The exponential dependency on <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is unavoidable computationally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer and Sandholm, 2006</a>)</cite>.
Further closing the gaps between upper and lower regret bounds is an open question and will likely involve tradeoff between different parameters and tradeoff between computation and regret.</p>
</div>
</section>
